{"surface": "inspector", "title": "Full text", "html": "<header class=\"hero\"><p class=\"kicker\">Full text</p><h2 class=\"title\">benzinga.com</h2><p class=\"ctx\"><a href=\"https://www.benzinga.com/markets/equities/26/11/63320949/tech-stocks-960-billion-china-ai-chips-remote-access-rule\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">benzinga.com/markets/equities/26/11/63320949/tech-stocks-960-billion-china-ai-chips-remote-access-rule</a> · read Nov 19, 2026</p></header><div class=\"page md\" data-md=\"# Tech Stocks Wipe Out $960 Billion as Washington Shuts China&#x27;s AI Chip Back Door in 5 Countries\n\nFor years, Chinese companies did not need to smuggle [America’s most powerful AI chips](https://www.benzinga.com/markets/equities/26/11/63290031/nvidia-jensen-huang-profumo-espionage-risk-adam-irwin-heligan). A rented server in Southeast Asia was enough.\n\nOn Monday, Washington moved to lock that door, and the bill landed on Wall Street.\n\nThe Commerce Department’s Bureau of Industry and Security issued an interim final rule that requires a license to ship advanced AI chips to Indonesia, Malaysia, Singapore, Thailand and Brazil. Washington says these five countries “present a higher-than-normal risk of diversion.”\n\nBy 2:40 p.m. ET, 19 of the largest U.S.-listed technology stocks had lost about \\$960 billion in combined market value.\n\n**Nvidia Corp.** [(NASDAQ:NVDA)](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/NVDA) took the biggest hit. The stock fell 4.4% to \\$155.70, erasing roughly \\$172 billion in its first full week without **Jensen Huang** at the helm.\n\nThe **VanEck Semiconductor ETF** [(NASDAQ:SMH)](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/SMH) slid 4.7%. The Nasdaq 100 dropped 2.4% and the S&amp;P 500 fell 1.9%.\n\n**Read Also: [EXCLUSIVE: Huang&#x27;s Exit Echoes Profumo, but Intelligence Expert Says Chip CEOs Are the New Spy Targets](https://www.benzinga.com/markets/equities/26/11/63290031/nvidia-jensen-huang-profumo-espionage-risk-adam-irwin-heligan)**\n\n## **How the Cloud Back Door Worked**\n\nSince 2022, Chinese companies have largely been barred from buying Nvidia’s most advanced chips.\n\nRenting them was a different story. Data centers in Southeast Asia could buy the hardware legally and then sell the computing power to customers in China, who never had to touch a single chip.\n\nThe new rule bars shipments to any data center, anywhere in the world, that gives companies headquartered in China, Macau or other arms-embargoed countries access to more than 12 million units of “aggregate TPP.”\n\nTotal processing performance is the yardstick Washington uses to measure raw computing power.\n\nData centers that already serve those customers have 90 days to obtain a license. Operators are also expected to check who ultimately owns their clients, and a customer that refuses to answer is treated as a red flag.\n\n“The rule is designed to ensure that existing restrictions on access to sensitive American technology cannot be circumvented through remote computing arrangements involving data centers in third countries,” the Bureau of Industry and Security said in a statement.\n\n## **Treasury Turns on Commerce**\n\nThe rule landed three days after Treasury Secretary **Scott Bessent** took a fight inside the administration public.\n\nNvidia’s board accepted **Jensen Huang**‘s resignation on Nov. 11, after he admitted to an affair with **Alice Huang**, a former executive at Singapore-based data center company **Megaspeed** whom U.S. officials are still investigating over whether she is a Chinese intelligence asset.\n\nBessent said four agencies had wanted Megaspeed on the U.S. trade blacklist for months.\n\n“Treasury, War, USTR, and State, they were in favor of putting Megaspeed on the entity list for months,” Bessent said Friday. “But Commerce has the pen, Commerce BIS has the pen, and you will have to ask them about their internal processes.”\n\nThe Bureau of Industry and Security, the agency that wrote Monday’s rule, sits inside **Howard Lutnick**‘s Commerce Department.\n\nOn Sunday, Bessent turned to Nvidia itself.\n\n“They need a dramatic shake-up,” he said.\n\nHe also urged the board to look past internal candidates. “This makes me think that as the board conducts a search for a new leader, that they need to go outside the company,” Bessent said.\n\nThe White House has said President **Donald Trump** “maintains complete confidence” in Lutnick, who has agreed to testify before the Senate Banking Committee.\n\nBank of America had seen this coming.\n\n“The Nvidia gate opens a can of worms that goes beyond the immediate pressure on the stock market,” Bank of America global economists **Claudio Irigoyen** and **Antonio Gabriel** said in a report Monday.\n\n“Jensen Huang’s departure will likely trigger heightened geopolitical tensions between the US and China, and an eventual motion for tighter export controls across chips and rare earths,” they added.\n\n**Read Also: [Nvidia Gets 10 Bank Downgrades After Huang Exit: What to Expect Ahead of Q3 Results](https://www.benzinga.com/analyst-stock-ratings/downgrades/26/11/63290113/nvidia-downgrades-huang-exit-third-quarter-earnings-preview)**\n\n## **Even the Exempt Got Hit**\n\nOn paper, America’s cloud giants are spared. The license requirement for the five countries does not apply to customers headquartered in the United States, as long as exporters file the new reports the rule requires.\n\nInvestors sold them anyway.\n\n**Alphabet Inc.** [(NASDAQ:GOOGL)](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/GOOGL) fell 3.8%, losing about \\$135 billion, while **Amazon.com Inc.** [(NASDAQ:AMZN)](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/AMZN) shed 3.5%, or roughly \\$101 billion.\n\nThe steepest drops came from the companies that sell the memory, storage and plumbing behind every AI server. **Sandisk Corp.** [(NASDAQ:SNDK)](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/SNDK) sank 8.3%, **Arm Holdings plc** [(NASDAQ:ARM)](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/ARM) fell 7.8% and **Micron Technology Inc.** [(NASDAQ:MU)](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/MU) dropped 7.1%.\n\n## **The AI Selloff Leaderboard**\n\n| Company | Change | Market value lost |\n|----|----|----|\n| **Nvidia Corp.** [(NASDAQ:NVDA)](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/NVDA) | -4.4% | ~\\$172 billion |\n| **Alphabet Inc.** [(NASDAQ:GOOGL)](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/GOOGL) | -3.8% | ~\\$135 billion |\n| **Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Co. Ltd.** [(NYSE:TSM)](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/TSM) | -5.8% | ~\\$114 billion |\n| **Amazon.com Inc.** [(NASDAQ:AMZN)](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/AMZN) | -3.5% | ~\\$101 billion |\n| **Broadcom Inc.** [(NASDAQ:AVGO)](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/AVGO) | -4.1% | ~\\$68 billion |\n| **Micron Technology Inc.** [(NASDAQ:MU)](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/MU) | -7.1% | ~\\$63 billion |\n| **Advanced Micro Devices Inc.** [(NASDAQ:AMD)](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/AMD) | -5.3% | ~\\$47 billion |\n| **ASML Holding N.V.** [(NASDAQ:ASML)](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/ASML) | -5.1% | ~\\$31 billion |\n| **Intel Corp.** [(NASDAQ:INTC)](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/INTC) | -7.0% | ~\\$22 billion |\n| **Oracle Corp.** [(NYSE:ORCL)](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/ORCL) | -5.3% | ~\\$20 billion |\n\n*Source: [Benzinga Pro](https://www.benzinga.com/pro/register-3?utm_content=editorial&amp;utm_ad_type=content-text), Benzinga calculations. Updated at 2:40 p.m. ET on Nov. 16, 2026.*\n\n*Image: Shutterstock*\" data-base=\"https://www.benzinga.com/markets/equities/26/11/63320949/tech-stocks-960-billion-china-ai-chips-remote-access-rule\"></div><p class=\"raw\"><a class=\"rawlink\" data-go=\"raw:src:81\">Full details</a></p>", "wide": true}